    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion earlier. Let's focus on the legal aspect of the residuals clause and the term \"unaided memory.\"\n\nIn a legal context, \"unaided memory\" refers to the information that an individual retains in their mind without the aid of any external tools, such as notes, recordings, or other forms of documentation. It's the knowledge that you have internalized through experience or study, which you can recall and use without having to refer back to any physical or digital records.\n\nRegarding the residuals clause in your NDA, it allows you to use the knowledge you've gained from working with the confidential information, as long as you don't disclose the confidential information itself. This means that if you remember an algorithm or a method for optimizing database queries that you learned while working for the company, you can use that knowledge in your future work, even with other clients, because it's part of your unaided memory.\n\nHowever, it's important to note that the specific language of the NDA and the jurisdiction in which it is enforced can affect the interpretation of such clauses. If the residuals clause is written in a way that restricts the use of knowledge gained from the confidential information, even if it's in your unaided memory, then there could be a risk of",
